cff-version: 1.2.1
message: "If you refer to this work, please cite it as below."
title: "triptych: a Prolog realisation of Sun, a system poem"
abstract: >-
  A Prolog realisation of the system poem Sun. A ten-word grid — three
  nouns, three verbs, four adverbs — permuted across two syntactic orders
  yields seventy-two variants, one for each eclipse of Saros 126
  (1179–2459). The 2026 eclipse is the forty-eighth of that cycle.
  Prolog is chosen for its declarative architecture, which states the
  constraints of a system rather than the steps to execute them, after
  the glossematics of Louis Hjelmslev. Nine of the ten words are
  referentially autonomous; the tenth, now, is deictic, and its meaning
  is constituted at the moment of utterance. It occurs in eighteen of
  the seventy-two variants, dividing the grid at exactly one quarter of
  its outputs into the readable-from-anywhere and the
  readable-only-from-here. The work is structurally isomorphic to the
  Antikythera Mechanism, built from words rather than bronze, and
  deliberately placeless where that machine was bound to a longitude.
